# Data visualisation

Data visualisation turns readings into lines, shapes and colours that people understand quickly. In Home Assistant that might be the power the house is using now, how warm each room was overnight, or where everyone’s phone last reported.

Design every chart, dashboard and map around a decision the household needs to make, such as turning the heating down or checking why the freezer used more energy this week.

## Pages in this section

- [Charts](brand-data-charts.html): the parts of a chart, keeping readings true and giving a text alternative.
- [Dashboards](brand-data-dashboards.html): when a dashboard helps, and how to keep reports apart from live cards.
- [Maps](brand-data-maps.html): when to use a map, and how to make it clear and accessible.
- [Data examples](brand-data-examples.html): working energy and climate charts for invented households.

## Principles

Follow these 5 principles for every chart, dashboard and map.

1. **Clarity.** Show only what the household needs for the task.
2. **Accessibility.** Give everyone the same information. Do not use colour alone, and give the data as a table or text.
3. **Accuracy.** Show readings as Home Assistant recorded them, with gaps shown as gaps. Say where the data comes from.
4. **Consistency.** Give each device the same colour, name and unit everywhere.
5. **Engagement.** Make the important reading easy to find, so people can act on it.

<h2 id="bg-dat-01">Tell a clear story</h2>

Before you choose a chart, write down what the person needs to know, such as which room is coldest or which sensor is unavailable.

1. **Know the purpose.** Decide who will look at it. Someone checking the heating needs less detail than someone tuning an automation.
2. **Know the story.** Decide the one thing to show, such as “the hall gets cold after midnight”.
3. **Know what matters.** Remove anything that does not help. Put the main reading where people look first, without a click.

Do not write a reassuring message when readings are missing.

<h2 id="bg-dat-02">Choose a visualisation</h2>

Choose the chart from the relationship in the data. This table suggests a chart for each relationship, with the Home Assistant card that can draw it.

| Relationship | Home Assistant example | Chart to use | In Home Assistant |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Time | Living room temperature over the last 24 hours | Line chart | History graph card or Statistics graph card |
| Magnitude | Energy used by each circuit today | Column or bar chart | Statistics graph card, as a bar chart |
| Rank | Devices ordered by energy used this week | Horizontal bar chart | Devices energy graph on the Energy dashboard |
| Deviation | Each room’s temperature against its target | Bar chart or dot plot | Thermostat card for one room. A table for several rooms. |
| Part-to-whole | Today’s electricity from the grid, solar panels and battery | Stacked bar or a table of shares | Energy usage graph or a table |
| Flow | Energy moving from solar panels to the battery and the home | Flow diagram | Energy distribution card, or the Sankey card where available |
| Distribution | How many nights the bedroom was below 16°C, 16 to 18°C or above 18°C this month | Column chart of ranges | No native card. Use a table. |
| Correlation | Outdoor temperature against how long the boiler ran each day | Scatter plot | No native card. Use a table. |
| Spatial | Where each household member’s phone last reported | Map | Map card |

Keep power (W or kW) and energy (Wh or kWh) on separate charts. [Energy flow and other chart families](../home-assistant/energy-flow-and-other-charts.html) owns the energy distribution diagram, gauges and Sankey cards. [Maps](brand-data-maps.html) explains when a map is the right choice.
